{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1792275270776,"sessionID":"ses_eb4120070ffeR1Qk1nw540OQZ3","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"websearch","callID":"call_00_odhKVCxDvfo5NeI7DVGk9294","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"query":"\"both the number of features F and ingredient embedding dimension d using the set\" GISMo arxiv","numResults":5},"output":"Title: [2302.07960] Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\nURL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.07960\nPublished: 2023-02-15T04:11:12.000Z\nAuthor: Fatemi, Bahare, Duval, Quentin, Girdhar, Rohit, Drozdzal, Michal, Romero-Soriano, Adriana\nHighlights:\nallergens, and ease culinary exploration in everyone's kitchen. To address ingredient substitution, we build a benchmark, composed of a dataset of substitution pairs with standardized splits, evaluation metrics, and baselines. We further introduce\n\n---\n\nTitle: Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\nURL: https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2302.07960\nPublished: 2023-02-15T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Bahare Fatemi, Quentin Duval, Rohit Girdhar, Michal Drozdzal, Adriana Romero\nHighlights:\n## GISMo Implementation Details\n\nWe implemented our model in PyTorch [44], used deep graph library (DGL) [57] for the sparse operations, and used Adam [30] as the optimizer. We performed early stopping and hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the vali dation set for all models, and used a maximum number of epochs of 1000. We tuned the learning rate lr using the set { 0.005, 0.001, 0.0005, 0.0001, 0.00005, 0.00001, 0.000005} , and both the number of features F and ingredient embed ding dimension d using the set { 200, 300, 400, 500, 600} . Our final model consists of an ingredient encoder (IE) composed of two GIN [59] layers, and an ingredient substi tution decoder (ISD) composed of a multi-layer perceptron with three layers. The model uses F = 300. The input ingredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredi ent embedding dimension is set to d = 300. The model is trained with a learning rate lr = 0.00005, and is regularized using a weight decay of 0.0001 and a dropout rate of 0.25.\n\n---\n\nTitle: [2302.07960] Learning to Substitute Ingredients in Recipes\nURL: https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2302.07960\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## Appendix A GISMo Implementation Details\n\nWe implemented our model in PyTorch [44], used deep graph library (DGL) [57] for the sparse operations, and used Adam [30] as the optimizer. We performed early stopping and hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the validation set for all models, and used a maximum number of epochs of $1000$ . We tuned the learning rate $lr$ using the set $\\{0.005,0.001,0.0005,0.0001,0.00005,0.00001,0.000005\\}$ , and both the number of features $F$ and ingredient embedding dimension $d$ using the set $\\{200,300,400,500,600\\}$ .\n\nOur final model consists of an ingredient encoder ( $\\mathrm{IE}$ ) composed of two GIN [59] layers, and an ingredient substitution decoder ( $\\mathrm{ISD}$ ) composed of a multi-layer perceptron with three layers. The model uses $F=300$ . The input ingredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredient embedding dimension is set to $d=300$ . The model is trained with a learning rate $lr=0.00005$ , and is regularized using a weight decay of $0.0001$ and a dropout rate of $0.25$ .\n\n---\n\nTitle: N/A\nURL: https://export.arxiv.org/pdf/2302.07960v1.pdf\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n#### A. GISMo Implementation Details\n\nWe implemented our model in PyTorch [44], used deep graph library (DGL) [57] for the sparse operations, and used Adam [30] as the optimizer. We performed early stopping and hyperparameter tuning based on the MRR on the vali dation set for all models, and used a maximum number of epochs of 1000. We tuned the learning rate lr using the set { 0.005, 0.001, 0.0005, 0.0001, 0.00005, 0.00001, 0.000005} , and both the number of features F and ingredient embed ding dimension d using the set { 200, 300, 400, 500, 600} . Our final model consists of an ingredient encoder (IE) composed of two GIN [59] layers, and an ingredient substi tution decoder (ISD) composed of a multi-layer perceptron with three layers. The model uses F = 300. The input ingredient features are one-hot encodings and the ingredi ent embedding dimension is set to d = 300. The model is trained with a learning rate lr = 0.00005, and is regularized using a weight decay of 0.0001 and a dropout rate of 0.25.\n\n---\n\nTitle: N/A\nURL: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2302.07960\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nMartin-Bautista. A word embedding-based method for un\u0002supervised adaptation of cooking recipes. IEEE Access,\n9:27389–27404, 2021. 1, 8\n[40] Taichi Nishimura, Atsushi Hashimoto, and Shinsuke Mori.\nProcedural text generation from a photo sequence. In Pro\u0002ceedings of the 12th International Conference on Natural\nLanguage Generation, pages 409–414, Tokyo, Japan, Oct.–\nNov. 2019. Association for Computational Linguistics. 8\n[41] Siyuan Pan, Ling Dai, Xuhong Hou, Huating Li, and Bin\nSheng. Chefgan: Food image generation from recipes. In\nProceedings of the 28th ACM International Conference on\nMultimedia, MM ’20, page 4244–4252, New York, NY,\nUSA, 2020. Association for Computing Machinery. 8\n[42] Yuran Pan, Qiangwen Xu, and Yanjun Li. Food recipe alter\u0002nation and generation with natural language processing tech\u0002niques. In 2020 IEEE 36th International Conference on Data\nEngineering Workshops (ICDEW), pages 94–97, 2020. 8\n[43] Donghyeon Park, Keonwoo Kim, Seoyoon Kim, Michael\nSpranger, and Jaewoo Kang. Flavorgraph: a large-scale\nfood-chemical graph for generating food representations and\nrecommending food pairings. Scientific reports, 11(1):1–13,\n2021. 1, 4, 5, 6, 8\n[44] Adam Paszke, Sam Gross, Soumith Chintala, Gregory\nChanan, Edward Yang, Zachary DeVito, Zeming Lin, Al\u0002ban Desmaison, Luca Antiga, and Adam Lerer. Automatic\ndifferentiation in pytorch. 2017. 4, 11\